# Under leapfrog every program of a step leaves the same state: a step
# that writes a row of energies is the plain step to the bit (#285). The
# dipeptide in water, 20 steps in the deterministic mode on the CPU; two
# runs that differ only in 'energy_interval' end in the same state, at
# constant energy, with velocity rescaling, a Nose-Hoover chain, and
# Langevin dynamics, with and without constraints, and with stochastic
# cell rescaling by each count of its work ("TROTTER",
# "TROTTER_FIRST_ORDER", "EXACT", "FIRST_ORDER") and each coupling of the
# cell, whose step that closes a period takes or leaves the energies. In
# double precision the positions are those of velocity Verlet within
# 1e-11 nm, where it takes the same steps. Inputs/leapfrog_energy_step.py.
#
# All 17 cases run here in double precision: the programs of a step are
# written once for the CPU and a GPU and for both precisions, so every
# suite checks every case here. In mixed precision three cases run by
# default, as on a GPU (leapfrog-energy-step-gpu.test); all 17 run there
# in the suite of a release (leapfrog-energy-step-release.test and its
# -gpu twin, lit's -Drelease=1).
#
# The three cases, one for each form of the step with constraints (#285):
#   langevin               Langevin dynamics: the velocities change within
#                          the drift, so every program takes the projected
#                          form, the first half kick of velocity Verlet;
#   nve                    constant energy: the step with the virial keeps
#                          the kick of the plain step and takes the drift
#                          of velocity Verlet on the side, for the virial;
#   v-rescale-anisotropic  the scaling step of the barostat with
#                          "TROTTER_FIRST_ORDER" and the anisotropic
#                          coupling, with against without the virial.
# Before the fix the two runs of each of the three differed (by 2.7e-6,
# 2.3e-14, and 4.8e-6 nm on the CPU in double precision).
#
# REQUIRES: hdf5
#
# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/leapfrog_energy_step.py mdir %S/Inputs %t/double CPU DOUBLE | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=DOUBLE
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/leapfrog_energy_step.py mdir %S/Inputs %t/mixed CPU MIXED langevin nve v-rescale-anisotropic | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MIXED
#
# With constraints, where the velocities change within the drift, every
# program takes the first half kick of velocity Verlet from the projected
# velocities of the time of the positions: under Langevin dynamics the
# plain step as well.
# RUN: sed -e 's|"VELOCITY_VERLET"|"LEAPFROG"|' -e 's|^ensemble    = "NVE"|ensemble = "NVT"\ntemperature = 300.0\n[thermostat]\nmethod = "LANGEVIN"\nfriction = 1.0|' \
# RUN:     -e "s|ff19.prmtop|%S/Inputs/ff19sb/ff19.prmtop|" -e "s|ff19.inpcrd|%S/Inputs/ff19sb/ff19.inpcrd|" \
# RUN:     %S/leapfrog-constraints.test | sed -n '/^#--- run.toml/,/^#--- end/p' | sed '1d;$d' > %t/langevin.toml
# RUN: mdir emit %t/langevin.toml | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LANGEVIN

# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE nve: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE nve-free: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE v-rescale: the states are identical
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE nose-hoover: the states are identical
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE langevin: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE langevin-free: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE langevin-trotter: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE langevin-trotter-first-order: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE v-rescale-trotter: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE v-rescale-trotter-first-order: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE v-rescale-exact: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE v-rescale-first-order: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE nose-hoover-trotter: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE v-rescale-semi-isotropic: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
# DOUBLE: CPU DOUBLE v-rescale-anisotropic: the states are identical; within {{.*}} nm of velocity Verlet
